1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,600
Part 4 Hi everyone, in this session I'll be presenting my research about the social history of Britain during the Industrial Revolution.

2
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:16,160
I particularly looked at how ordinary lives were affected by changes that happened at that time.

3
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:20,240
This was a time that saw the beginning of a new phenomenon:

4
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:27,200
consumerism - where buying and selling goods became a major part of ordinary people's lives.

5
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:31,960
In fact, it was in the 19th century that the quantity

6
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:37,400
and quality of people's possessions was used as an indication of the wealth of the country.

7
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,480
Before this, the vast majority of people had very few possessions,

8
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,320
but all that was changed by the Industrial Revolution.

9
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:51,560
This was the era from the mid-18th to the late 19th century,

10
00:00:51,900 --> 00:01:02,380
when improvements in how goods were made as well as in technology triggered massive social changes that transformed life for just about everybody in several key areas.

11
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:06,080
First let's look at manufacturing.

12
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:08,680
When it comes to manufacturing,

13
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:14,800
we tend to think of the Industrial Revolution in images of steam engines and coal.

14
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:19,520
And it's true that the Industrial Revolution couldn't have taken place at all

15
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:22,200
if it weren't for these new sources of power.

16
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:26,660
They marked an important shift away from the traditional watermills

17
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,240
and windmills that had dominated before this.

18
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:35,980
The most advanced industry for much of the 19th century was textiles.

19
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:38,880
This meant that fashionable fabrics

20
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:42,400
and lace and ribbons were made available to everyone.

21
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:49,420
Before the Industrial Revolution, most people made goods to sell in small workshops

22
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:51,600
often in their own homes.

23
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:57,480
But enormous new machines were now being created that could produce the goods faster

24
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,620
and on a larger scale,

25
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,440
and these required a lot more space.

26
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:05,340
So large factories were built

27
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,860
replacing the workshops, and forcing workers to travel to work.

28
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:16,480
In fact, large numbers of people migrated from villages into towns as a result.

29
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:21,620
As well as manufacturing, there were new technologies in transport,

30
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,440
contributing to the growth of consumerism.

31
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:29,240
The horse-drawn stagecoaches and carts of the 18th century,

32
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:31,780
which carried very few people and goods

33
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:35,100
and travelled slowly along poorly surfaced roads,

34
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:39,020
were gradually replaced by the numerous canals that were constructed.

35
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,060
These were particularly important for the transportation of goods.

36
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,580
The canals gradually fell out of use, though, as railways were developed,

37
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,200
becoming the main way of moving goods

38
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,760
and people from one end of the country to the other.

39
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:00,460
And the goods they moved weren't just coal, iron, clothes,

40
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:04,840
and so on - significantly, they included newspapers,

41
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:10,980
which meant that thousands of people were not only more knowledgeable about what was going on in the country,

42
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:14,940
but could also read about what was available in the shops.

43
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,160
And that encouraged them to buy more.

44
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:24,800
So faster forms of transport resulted in distribution becoming far more efficient -

45
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:30,700
goods could now be sold all over the country, instead of just in the local market.

46
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:38,500
The third main area that saw changes that contributed to consumerism was retailing.

47
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:42,300
The number and quality of shops grew rapidly,

48
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:49,300
and in particular, small shops suffered as customers flocked to the growing number of department stores -

49
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:53,280
a form of retailing that was new in the 19th century.

50
00:03:54,280 --> 00:04:01,500
The entrepreneurs who opened these found new ways to stock them with goods, and to attract customers:

51
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:10,220
for instance, improved lighting inside greatly increased the visibility of the goods for sale.

52
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:17,860
Another development that made goods more visible from outside resulted from the use of plate glass,

53
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:22,280
which made it possible for windows to be much larger than previously.

54
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,360
New ways of promoting goods were introduced, too.

55
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:33,460
Previously, the focus had been on informing potential customers about the availability of goods;

56
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:40,020
now there was an explosion in advertising trying to persuade people to go shopping.

57
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:47,420
Flanders claims that one of the great effects of the Industrial Revolution was that it created choice.

58
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:51,460
All sorts of things that had previously been luxuries

59
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:55,580
from sugar to cutlery - became conveniences,

60
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:00,060
and before long they'd turned into necessities

61
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:04,200
life without sugar or cutlery was unimaginable.

62
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:06,860
Rather like mobile phones these days!
